# Hash notes — the ephemeral lane

There are two kinds of prose in a `.work` file. The first is durable: it describes
the system as it timelessly *is*, and it's read as documentation. The second is
about the *change* — "I switched this because X broke", "TODO verify on prod",
"next: wire the guest shim". That second kind doesn't belong in your documentation,
because it goes stale the moment the work moves on. A **hash note** is its home.

Keeping the two apart matters: prose is *trusted*. The moment edit-talk leaks into
it, the documentation starts to lie. Hash notes give that change-story a place to
live where it stays useful — and out of the prose.

## The three forms

A hash note is a single line in its own lane. The first character names what it is:

- **`+# …`** — a **pinned** note. It stays expanded on the page (the "desk") and
  survives sweeps. Use it for in-flight intent the next person should see.
- **`-# …`** — a **drawered** note. On the next sweep it collapses to a `≡<hash>`
  handle: the prose moves to the drawer and a small reference is left in its place.
- **`≡<hash> …`** — a **collapsed handle**. The note isn't gone — this is a live
  anchor you can re-expand at any time.

Notes **nest** by indentation, so a tall stack can roll up under one parent handle
instead of cluttering the page.

## Nothing is ever lost

Hash notes are **lossless by design**. A drawered note doesn't vanish — it moves
down a ladder, never off it:

> **desk** (expanded in source) → **drawer** (a `≡<hash>` handle + a stored row) →
> **archive** (git history)

The `≡<hash>` is a short prefix of the commit that swept the note, so the handle
*is* a real git ref — `git show <hash>` recovers the note even if the drawer row is
later evicted. To put a note away you flip `+#` to `-#`; to throw one away you have
to delete it deliberately, and even then git still has it. The system makes losing
context hard on purpose.

## Reading them back

From the CLI, the `work note` family explores the whole note repository — and it's
git-backed, so it works without a running nexus:

- `work note list` — every note across the tree.
- `work note grep <text>` — search notes by content.
- `work note near <file>` — notes anchored to a file you're reading.
- `work note expand <hash>` — re-expand a collapsed handle to its full text.

Agents get the same context automatically: when one is about to edit a file, the
collapsed notes nearby are surfaced first — so a caution a previous agent recorded
("don't revert this, it deadlocked the runtime") is read *before* the mistake is
repeated, not after.

## Guardrails

A drawered note can graduate from a footnote into an active check. Tag it `#guard`
and give it a `/pattern/`:

```
-# #guard read secrets through Nexus.Secrets, never the env /System\.get_env/
```

Once swept, that note becomes a tripwire: anything scanning a diff against the
drawer will fire on a match and surface the original note. The lesson defends the
codebase instead of waiting to be re-learned.

## When is something a hash note, not prose?

If any of these is true, it's a hash note:

- **Subject** — it's about the *change or decision*, not the *thing itself*.
- **Staleness** — it'll be false after the next edit ("now uses vfkit").
- **Deixis** — it leans on *now / was / changed / TODO / next / I*.

When you genuinely can't tell, prefer the hash note. A note costs almost nothing
(it's lossless and re-expandable), but a stale sentence in your prose rots the
documentation everyone trusts. Keep prose pure; let the change-story live in the
drawer.

Hash notes never reach a woven workbook — they're a tool for the people and agents
writing the source, stripped from the shipped artifact. See also the durable lane:
[Prose & refs](/language/prose-and-refs).
